+ P004: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for clinical documentation, patient communication, and other language tasks. Many such applications begin by extracting relevant facts from electronic health record (EHR) notes before those facts can be summarized or reused. Reliable note extraction is therefore a practical requirement for applying LLMs to oncology care and research.
+ P006: Although EHRs contain structured fields, much clinically relevant information appears in free-text narratives (Kong, 2019; Wiest et al., 2024). Oncology notes often combine diagnoses, prior and planned treatments, test results, and changing disease status across sections and time points. Uncertainty, copied-forward text, and redundant documentation make the current clinical state difficult to separate from historical information. Outpatient notes have also become longer and more redundant over time, in part because of templates and copied text (Rule et al., 2021).
+ P008: LLMs can extract information from these notes, but fluent output may omit facts, confuse timelines, or add plausible statements that the note does not support. Commercial models have shown variable performance on clinical extraction tasks. Their use can also raise privacy and governance questions when protected health information is sent to externally hosted services. Local deployment keeps note processing within the institution, but it does not by itself prevent extraction errors.
+ P010: Extraction performance depends on both the model and the workflow used at inference. Fine-tuning can adapt a model to a clinical task, but it requires suitable training data and additional development. Prompt design, task decomposition, verification, and deterministic clinical rules offer another route that does not change model weights. Prior work has used iterative prompt refinement and clinical review to improve structured extraction (Huang et al., 2024; Hein et al., 2025). Less is known about how to convert recurring expert feedback into a reproducible inference workflow for complex oncology notes.
+ P012: We use the term inference harness for the workflow that surrounds a frozen LLM: the sequence of extraction steps, verification checks, and clinical rules applied at inference. We developed a locally deployable harness for oncology-note extraction, refined it first on breast-cancer notes with physician input, and then adapted it to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma notes through model-assisted review without physician review of pancreatic outputs. We compared the final harness with a single-prompt baseline using the same underlying model and field definitions, with system identities masked during physician evaluation. We also conducted an exploratory staged technical ablation to examine how successive harness components changed output quality.

