@@ Discussion, Limitations / P119 @@
- This study has several limitations. First, our primary outcome was physician preference rather than absolute extraction accuracy. A tie could indicate that both systems produced acceptable outputs or that both contained errors. Although physician ratings consistently favored the harness, the evaluation included only four physicians and 40 clinical notes from two cancer types within a single-source dataset. However, these were real clinical notes rather than synthetic cases or model-generated narratives. They contain repeated, copied-forward information, uncertain findings, treatment plans, or even typographical errors that make the extraction task challenging. Nevertheless, further studies are needed to investigate whether the results can be generalized to other cancer types or language models. Second, the exploratory ablation was evaluated by a single non-physician LLM judge and compared cumulative stages rather than individual components. Therefore, it could not determine the independent contribution of each verification step or clinical rule.
+ This study has several limitations. The primary outcome was physician preference rather than absolute extraction accuracy, and a tie could mean that both outputs were acceptable or that both contained errors. The evaluation included four physicians and 40 notes from two cancer types in one source dataset. The development status of the 40 benchmark note texts requires explicit provenance reporting because project records indicate that the texts may have informed error analysis even though the expert annotations were not used in the primary endpoint. The evaluation interface also used fixed A/B positions, and source attribution was available only in the harness output. These features may have allowed evaluators to infer a recurring pattern or may have influenced preference, but the direction and magnitude of any effect cannot be determined. Both systems used the same base-model family, so the results do not establish generalization to other model families. Finally, the exploratory staged technical ablation used one non-physician LLM judge and compared cumulative stages. It therefore could not determine the independent contribution of each verification step or clinical rule.

